      "t": "Я вынужден тебя обыскать.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты при всех уже воруешь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are already stealing in front of everyone.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'you' and finishing on 'steal'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А если тебя сейчас поймают?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what if they catch you right now?' Fedya brings out the contrast between 'and' and 'if' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ворую у богатых, бедным отдаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I steal from the rich and give to the poor.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'steal' and finishing on 'give away'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "То есть отдаёшь только себе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is, you give it only to yourself.' Notice how Russian expresses 'that' and 'to oneself' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Lyova answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Так, а где филе ягнёнка?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, and where is the lamb fillet?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'so' before landing on 'lamb'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Сеня, ты его не видел?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Senya, have you not seen it?' In Russian, the idea of 'Senya' flows naturally into 'saw' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Чего сразу Сеня виноват?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why is Senya immediately to blame?' Senya raises the pitch on 'at fault' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Не видел я никакого филе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have not seen any fillet.' Senya brings out the contrast between 'not' and 'saw' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "А ты чего так нервничаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And why are you so nervous?' Lyova raises the pitch on 'are nervous' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Я совсем не нервничаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am not nervous at all.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'am nervous'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ты чего меня подозреваешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why do you suspect me?' Senya raises the pitch on 'suspect' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ты меня хоть раз ловил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Have you caught me even once?' Senya brings out the contrast between 'you' and 'me' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Я вынужден тебя обыскать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am forced to search you.' Notice how Russian expresses 'I' and 'search' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
